// Nexis Power Academy — Energy Advisor Capstone Certification Exam Bank
// "Nexis Energy Advisor" — advanced consultative certification, unlocked after
// Solar and HVAC certifications are complete. Focus: whole-home diagnosis,
// scenario reasoning, cross-service sequencing, and compliant consultative selling.
const EA_EXAM_BANK = [

  // ===================== SOLAR (10) =====================
  { id: 'ea-001', category: 'Solar', question: 'A homeowner asks how a solar system\'s production is measured before a quote is finalized. Which factor most directly determines the recommended system size?', choices: ['The color of the customer\'s roof shingles', 'The number of bedrooms in the home', 'The customer\'s current and anticipated future electricity usage', 'The customer\'s preferred monthly payment amount'], answerIndex: 2, explain: 'System sizing should be based on actual and anticipated electricity consumption, not cosmetic or unrelated factors like payment preference alone.' },
  { id: 'ea-002', category: 'Solar', question: 'A customer with a 22-year-old roof asks to move straight to a solar installation quote. What should the advisor do first?', choices: ['Schedule a professional roof inspection before finalizing a solar recommendation', 'Proceed with the solar quote since panels can be removed and reinstalled later', 'Tell the customer the roof is fine since it hasn\'t leaked yet', 'Automatically include a full roof replacement in the solar contract'], answerIndex: 0, explain: 'Roof age and condition should be professionally evaluated before panels are installed, since re-roofing after installation adds cost and disruption.' },
  { id: 'ea-003', category: 'Solar', question: 'Which statement about solar production is accurate for advisor conversations?', choices: ['Solar panels produce a fixed, identical amount of energy every single day of the year', 'Solar production is unaffected by roof orientation or shading', 'Solar systems generate the same output at night as during the day', 'Solar production varies with sunlight, panel orientation, shading, and seasonal factors'], answerIndex: 3, explain: 'Production is variable and depends on multiple environmental and site factors, which is why annual estimates — not daily guarantees — are used.' },
  { id: 'ea-004', category: 'Solar', question: 'A customer asks whether their new solar system will completely eliminate their electric bill. What is the most appropriate advisor response?', choices: ['Confirm that their bill will be $0 once the system is installed', 'Explain that solar can significantly offset usage but a $0 bill cannot be guaranteed', 'Tell them bills are unrelated to solar production', 'Promise a specific dollar amount they will save every month'], answerIndex: 1, explain: 'Advisors must never guarantee a specific dollar savings or a $0 bill outcome — production and utility rate structures vary.' },
  { id: 'ea-005', category: 'Solar', question: 'What role does net metering or interconnection review typically play in a solar recommendation?', choices: ['It affects how excess solar production is credited and should be discussed as part of the proposal', 'It has no bearing on the customer\'s decision', 'It only matters for commercial solar customers', 'It is decided solely by the panel manufacturer'], answerIndex: 0, explain: 'Utility interconnection and net metering policies affect the value of solar production and should be part of an informed discussion.' },
  { id: 'ea-006', category: 'Solar', question: 'A customer wants to know how incentives and tax credits affect their solar cost. What is the compliant way to discuss this?', choices: ['Guarantee the exact rebate dollar amount the customer will receive', 'Tell the customer incentives never change and are guaranteed', 'Reference general incentive programs but advise the customer to verify eligibility and amounts with a tax professional or official source', 'Avoid mentioning incentives at all to prevent confusion'], answerIndex: 2, explain: 'Incentive and rebate amounts should never be guaranteed by the advisor without verification, since eligibility and program terms can change.' },
  { id: 'ea-007', category: 'Solar', question: 'Which of these is a legitimate reason a solar system might be recommended at a larger size than current usage suggests?', choices: ['The sales rep wants to increase the commission on the deal', 'The customer plans future electrification, such as an EV or heat pump, that will increase electricity demand', 'Larger systems are always cheaper per panel regardless of need', 'The customer\'s neighbor has a larger system'], answerIndex: 1, explain: 'Anticipated future electrical load, such as EV charging or heat pump conversion, is a legitimate reason to size a system above current usage alone.' },
  { id: 'ea-008', category: 'Solar', question: 'A customer\'s home has significant shading from mature trees on the south-facing roof plane. What is the appropriate advisor action?', choices: ['Ignore the shading since panels work the same in shade or sun', 'Refuse to discuss solar with this customer at all', 'Promise the shading will not affect production in any way', 'Discuss how shading affects production estimates and consider alternate roof planes or mitigation before finalizing a design'], answerIndex: 3, explain: 'Shading materially affects solar output and should be factored into design and production estimates rather than dismissed.' },
  { id: 'ea-009', category: 'Solar', question: 'Why is it important for an advisor to gather a customer\'s historical utility usage data (e.g., 12 months of bills) before proposing solar?', choices: ['It is only needed for financing paperwork, not sizing', 'One month of data is always sufficient for any home', 'Historical data is optional and rarely improves accuracy', 'Historical usage helps establish a more accurate baseline for sizing and production estimate accuracy'], answerIndex: 3, explain: 'A full year of usage data captures seasonal variation and gives a much more reliable baseline than a single month\'s snapshot.' },
  { id: 'ea-010', category: 'Solar', question: 'A customer asks if solar panels will still produce electricity on a cloudy day. What is the accurate answer?', choices: ['No, panels produce zero electricity unless skies are completely clear', 'Yes, at a reduced output compared to full sun conditions', 'Only if the customer has a battery installed', 'Cloud cover has no measurable effect on production'], answerIndex: 1, explain: 'Panels still generate electricity in diffuse light on cloudy days, just at reduced output relative to direct sun.' },

  // ===================== HVAC (10) =====================
  { id: 'ea-011', category: 'HVAC', question: 'What is the primary purpose of a Manual J load calculation before recommending HVAC equipment sizing?', choices: ['To determine the home\'s actual heating and cooling load so equipment is neither oversized nor undersized', 'To calculate the customer\'s monthly financing payment', 'To determine the color options available for the outdoor unit', 'To estimate the customer\'s solar production needs'], answerIndex: 0, explain: 'A Manual J (or equivalent) load calculation establishes accurate heating and cooling loads, which is the basis for correct equipment sizing.' },
  { id: 'ea-012', category: 'HVAC', question: 'A customer asks whether a bigger heat pump is always better for comfort. What should the advisor explain?', choices: ['Bigger units are always better because they heat and cool faster', 'Sizing does not matter as long as the brand is reputable', 'Oversized equipment can cause short-cycling and uneven comfort; correct sizing depends on a load calculation', 'Oversizing only affects the electric bill, never comfort'], answerIndex: 2, explain: 'Oversized systems short-cycle, leading to poor humidity control, uneven temperatures, and reduced equipment lifespan — correct sizing matters more than raw capacity.' },
  { id: 'ea-013', category: 'HVAC', question: 'Which factor should be evaluated alongside heat pump sizing before finalizing zone design?', choices: ['The customer\'s preferred thermostat brand only', 'The condition of the home\'s insulation and air sealing (building envelope)', 'The square footage of the garage', 'The color of the interior walls'], answerIndex: 1, explain: 'Poor insulation and air leakage change the real heat-loss/heat-gain profile of a home and can undermine an otherwise correctly sized system.' },
  { id: 'ea-014', category: 'HVAC', question: 'A customer asks the advisor to guarantee their new heat pump will keep the home exactly 70°F at -10°F outdoor temperatures. What is the appropriate response?', choices: ['Guarantee the exact temperature the customer requested', 'Tell the customer heat pumps cannot work in cold climates at all', 'Avoid answering and change the subject', 'Explain performance is based on verified load calculations and equipment specs, without guaranteeing an exact outcome'], answerIndex: 3, explain: 'Advisors should reference verified sizing and manufacturer performance data rather than making unverified comfort guarantees.' },
  { id: 'ea-015', category: 'HVAC', question: 'Why might replacing an oil furnace with a heat pump change a home\'s electricity usage profile?', choices: ['Heat pumps use no electricity at all', 'Electricity usage is unrelated to the heating fuel source', 'Heating load shifts from oil to electricity, increasing electric consumption while reducing oil use', 'Oil furnaces and heat pumps use identical amounts of electricity'], answerIndex: 2, explain: 'Electrifying heating shifts load from a fossil fuel to the electrical system, which is important to account for in any future solar sizing.' },
  { id: 'ea-016', category: 'HVAC', question: 'A customer\'s home has cold bedrooms and hot living rooms in summer with existing ductwork. What should the advisor investigate first?', choices: ['Whether the ductwork is properly sized/sealed and whether zoning or a ductless solution would address the imbalance', 'Immediately replace the entire HVAC system without further evaluation', 'Recommend closing vents in the living room permanently', 'Assume the thermostat is broken and replace it'], answerIndex: 0, explain: 'Uneven comfort is often a duct design, sealing, or zoning issue rather than an equipment capacity issue, and should be diagnosed before replacing equipment.' },
  { id: 'ea-017', category: 'HVAC', question: 'What is a legitimate reason to recommend a ductless mini-split system over a ducted heat pump?', choices: ['Ductless systems are always less expensive regardless of the home', 'Ducted systems are never appropriate for New England homes', 'The home lacks existing ductwork or has rooms that are difficult to heat/cool evenly with the current duct layout', 'Ductless systems require no professional installation'], answerIndex: 2, explain: 'Ductless mini-splits are often recommended for homes without ductwork or with persistent zone-comfort problems that ducted retrofits would struggle to solve cost-effectively.' },
  { id: 'ea-018', category: 'HVAC', question: 'A customer with an oil furnace asks if a heat pump can fully replace it in a Massachusetts winter. What is the most accurate, compliant answer?', choices: ['Modern cold-climate heat pumps can serve as primary heat for many homes, but the right configuration depends on verified load calculations and the customer\'s comfort goals', 'Heat pumps cannot function below freezing under any circumstances', 'A heat pump will always fully replace oil heat with no other considerations', 'Only oil heat works reliably in Massachusetts winters'], answerIndex: 0, explain: 'Cold-climate heat pumps can serve as primary heat, but sizing, backup strategy, and comfort goals should be verified rather than assumed universally.' },
  { id: 'ea-019', category: 'HVAC', question: 'Before finalizing HVAC zone assumptions in a home with visibly under-insulated attic space, what should the advisor do?', choices: ['Ignore the insulation issue since it is outside the HVAC scope entirely', 'Assume the attic insulation has no bearing on heating load', 'Finalize the HVAC design first and address insulation only if the customer complains later', 'Flag insulation/weatherization as part of the diagnostic conversation before finalizing load assumptions'], answerIndex: 3, explain: 'Building envelope conditions affect heat-loss assumptions, so insulation should be part of the diagnostic conversation before finalizing HVAC design.' },
  { id: 'ea-020', category: 'HVAC', question: 'A customer just had a new high-efficiency HVAC system installed by Nexis last year and now asks about solar. What should the advisor do regarding HVAC?', choices: ['Recommend replacing the HVAC system again to pair better with solar', 'Confirm the existing HVAC system meets their needs rather than re-selling or replacing recently installed equipment', 'Assume the HVAC system is outdated without checking', 'Skip discussing HVAC and immediately upsell a second unit'], answerIndex: 1, explain: 'Consultative advising means not re-selling equipment that was recently and appropriately installed — the focus shifts to evaluating other needs.' },

  // ===================== DISCOVERY (12) =====================
  { id: 'ea-021', category: 'Discovery', question: 'Which of the following is NOT typically part of the Home Energy Discovery process?', choices: ['The customer\'s favorite streaming service', 'Current heating fuel and heating cost', 'Electrical service panel capacity', 'Future EV or renovation plans'], answerIndex: 0, explain: 'Discovery focuses on energy-relevant factors — heating fuel, electrical capacity, comfort issues, and future plans — not unrelated personal preferences.' },
  { id: 'ea-022', category: 'Discovery', question: 'A customer mentions they "might" buy an electric vehicle in the next 2-3 years. How should this be handled during discovery?', choices: ['Ignore it since the purchase is not confirmed', 'Immediately propose an EV charger installation', 'Record it as a relevant future load consideration that could affect electrical capacity and solar sizing', 'Tell the customer EVs are not compatible with solar homes'], answerIndex: 2, explain: 'Even tentative future plans like EV ownership are important discovery data points because they affect electrical capacity and future sizing decisions.' },
  { id: 'ea-023', category: 'Discovery', question: 'Why does an advisor ask about a customer\'s expected length of home ownership during discovery?', choices: ['It is used only for marketing mailing lists', 'It helps determine whether long-term investments like solar make sense versus shorter-term comfort fixes', 'It has no effect on which solutions are appropriate', 'It is required only for financing approval, not for advising'], answerIndex: 1, explain: 'Expected ownership duration affects payback-period relevance and whether long-term investments align with the customer\'s plans.' },
  { id: 'ea-024', category: 'Discovery', question: 'A customer says their basement feels damp and their upstairs bedrooms are always too warm. What discovery category does this fall under?', choices: ['Electrical service capacity', 'Roof age and condition', 'Future EV plans', 'Comfort problems and possibly building envelope/insulation issues'], answerIndex: 3, explain: 'Comfort complaints like temperature imbalance and dampness point toward comfort and building-envelope discovery areas, not electrical or roofing topics.' },
  { id: 'ea-025', category: 'Discovery', question: 'Which discovery question would help an advisor understand potential electrical capacity constraints before recommending electrification?', choices: ['What color are the kitchen cabinets?', 'How many pets live in the home?', 'What is the customer\'s preferred paint color for the exterior?', 'What is the current electrical panel size and how many amps is the service rated for?'], answerIndex: 3, explain: 'Panel size and service amperage are directly relevant to whether the home can support added electrical loads like heat pumps, EV chargers, or solar/battery.' },
  { id: 'ea-026', category: 'Discovery', question: 'A homeowner has no immediate interest in solar but mentions rising heating oil costs as their main frustration. What should the advisor prioritize in discovery?', choices: ['Immediately pivot to a solar sales pitch since that is the flagship product', 'Dig deeper into heating costs, fuel type, and comfort issues to identify the true underlying problem', 'Disregard the comment since the customer said no to solar', 'End the conversation since there is no clear sale'], answerIndex: 1, explain: 'The advisor should follow the customer\'s actual pain point — heating cost frustration — rather than defaulting to a product-first pitch.' },
  { id: 'ea-027', category: 'Discovery', question: 'What is the risk of skipping discovery and jumping straight to a product recommendation?', choices: ['The recommendation may not actually address the customer\'s real problem or may be inappropriately sized', 'There is no risk; discovery is optional for experienced advisors', 'Skipping discovery always results in a faster, better sale', 'Discovery is only useful for compliance paperwork, not for the recommendation itself'], answerIndex: 0, explain: 'Skipping diagnosis risks recommending a mismatched or improperly sized solution, undermining the consultative advising model.' },
  { id: 'ea-028', category: 'Discovery', question: 'During discovery, a customer reveals they are planning a kitchen renovation next year that will add significant square footage. How is this relevant?', choices: ['It is irrelevant to energy advising and should not be recorded', 'It only matters for the customer\'s contractor, not Nexis', 'It may affect future heating/cooling load and electrical demand, which should factor into sizing decisions', 'Renovation plans never affect HVAC or solar sizing'], answerIndex: 2, explain: 'Planned renovations that change square footage or layout can materially affect future heating, cooling, and electrical needs.' },
  { id: 'ea-029', category: 'Discovery', question: 'Which combination of discovery data points would be most useful for an advisor assessing whether a home is a good candidate for battery storage?', choices: ['The customer\'s favorite home decor style', 'Frequency of power outages in the area, existing solar interest, and critical loads the customer wants backed up', 'The number of houseplants in the home', 'The customer\'s commute distance to work'], answerIndex: 1, explain: 'Outage frequency, solar pairing, and desired backed-up loads are directly relevant to whether battery storage is worth evaluating.' },
  { id: 'ea-030', category: 'Discovery', question: 'A customer is vague about their monthly electric bill and says "it\'s pretty high, I guess." What should the advisor do next?', choices: ['Accept the vague answer and proceed with sizing estimates based on it', 'Assume a standard number without asking further', 'Skip the usage discussion entirely since the customer seems unsure', 'Request actual utility bills or usage data to get accurate figures rather than relying on a rough impression'], answerIndex: 3, explain: 'Accurate sizing and recommendations depend on real usage data, not vague impressions, so the advisor should request actual bills.' },
  { id: 'ea-031', category: 'Discovery', question: 'Why is it important to ask about a customer\'s cooling system (or lack thereof) even if they initially called about heating costs?', choices: ['Cooling systems are never relevant to a heating-focused conversation', 'Only commercial customers need cooling system discovery', 'Whole-home energy needs include both heating and cooling, and a missing AC system could point toward heat pump opportunities', 'Asking about cooling wastes time and should be avoided'], answerIndex: 2, explain: 'A whole-home approach considers both heating and cooling; a home without central AC may be a strong candidate for a heat pump that solves both needs.' },
  { id: 'ea-032', category: 'Discovery', question: 'What is the primary reason discovery includes questions about insulation and air leakage, not just equipment and usage?', choices: ['Building envelope conditions affect comfort and heat-loss assumptions that influence which solutions are appropriate', 'Insulation questions are only relevant to weatherization companies, not Nexis', 'Air leakage has no measurable effect on energy usage', 'Insulation is only discussed if the customer brings it up first'], answerIndex: 0, explain: 'Envelope conditions like insulation and air sealing directly affect comfort and load assumptions, making them essential discovery topics.' },

  // ===================== WHOLE-HOME ENERGY (12) =====================
  { id: 'ea-033', category: 'Whole-Home Energy', question: 'What is the core principle behind "whole-home energy thinking" as taught in the Energy Advisor curriculum?', choices: ['Every customer has a solar problem that solar panels will solve', 'Every customer has an HVAC problem that a new furnace will solve', 'A customer may have an energy, comfort, envelope, roof, electrical, or cost problem — diagnosis must happen before recommending a product', 'The advisor should recommend the highest-margin product first'], answerIndex: 2, explain: 'Whole-home thinking means diagnosing the actual underlying problem category before jumping to any specific product recommendation.' },
  { id: 'ea-034', category: 'Whole-Home Energy', question: 'A customer complains about high energy bills but their real issue turns out to be severe attic air leakage. What type of problem is this?', choices: ['A building envelope problem, not necessarily a solar or HVAC problem', 'Strictly a solar problem requiring more panels', 'Strictly an HVAC equipment problem requiring a new furnace', 'An electrical capacity problem'], answerIndex: 0, explain: 'Air leakage and insulation issues are building envelope problems — the right first step is weatherization, not immediately upgrading equipment.' },
  { id: 'ea-035', category: 'Whole-Home Energy', question: 'A customer wants an EV charger and a heat pump installed but their electrical panel is only rated for 100 amps with little spare capacity. What category of problem does this reflect?', choices: ['A roof problem', 'A comfort problem only', 'A problem that requires no further evaluation since panels are rarely a limiting factor', 'An electrical capacity problem that should be flagged for qualified electrical review'], answerIndex: 3, explain: 'Insufficient panel capacity for combined new loads is an electrical capacity problem that needs a licensed electrician\'s review before finalizing plans.' },
  { id: 'ea-036', category: 'Whole-Home Energy', question: 'Why does the Energy Advisor curriculum emphasize diagnosing the problem type before pitching a specific product?', choices: ['Because diagnosis is a regulatory requirement with no practical benefit', 'Because the wrong diagnosis leads to a mismatched, ineffective, or prematurely sold solution', 'Because product pitches are illegal without a diagnosis form', 'Because customers always know exactly which product they need already'], answerIndex: 1, explain: 'A mismatched recommendation driven by assumption rather than diagnosis wastes the customer\'s money and undermines trust and outcomes.' },
  { id: 'ea-037', category: 'Whole-Home Energy', question: 'A customer\'s home has excellent insulation, a new roof, and ample electrical capacity, but a very high electric bill from an all-electric baseboard heating system. What problem category best fits?', choices: ['An energy cost problem tied to inefficient heating equipment, worth evaluating for a heat pump', 'A roof problem requiring inspection', 'A building envelope problem requiring more insulation', 'No problem exists since insulation and roof are fine'], answerIndex: 0, explain: 'With envelope and roof already in good shape, the high cost points to inefficient heating equipment — a heat pump upgrade may directly address the cost problem.' },
  { id: 'ea-038', category: 'Whole-Home Energy', question: 'Which of these best illustrates a "comfort problem" as distinct from a pure cost problem?', choices: ['A home with an old, leaking roof', 'A home with no electrical panel capacity for expansion', 'A home where bills are moderate but certain rooms are consistently too cold or too hot', 'A home where the bill has doubled year over year'], answerIndex: 2, explain: 'Comfort problems relate to temperature consistency and livability, which can exist independent of overall cost, and often point to zoning or envelope issues.' },
  { id: 'ea-039', category: 'Whole-Home Energy', question: 'A customer says, "I just want my house to feel warmer in winter without spending a fortune." What should the advisor avoid doing?', choices: ['Asking clarifying discovery questions about their current heating system and comfort issues', 'Jumping immediately to a solar or HVAC equipment pitch without first diagnosing the comfort and cost drivers', 'Investigating whether insulation or air sealing is contributing to the discomfort', 'Explaining the range of possible solution categories before recommending one'], answerIndex: 1, explain: 'The advisor should diagnose before pitching — jumping straight to a product risks missing the actual root cause of the discomfort.' },
  { id: 'ea-040', category: 'Whole-Home Energy', question: 'Why might a "roof problem" take priority over a "solar opportunity" in an advisor\'s recommendation sequence?', choices: ['Roofs are never relevant to solar decisions', 'Roof problems are always more profitable to sell than solar', 'Solar panels extend the life of any roof regardless of condition', 'Installing solar on a roof nearing the end of its life risks costly panel removal/reinstallation later'], answerIndex: 3, explain: 'A failing roof should generally be addressed before solar installation to avoid the added cost and disruption of removing and reinstalling panels.' },
  { id: 'ea-041', category: 'Whole-Home Energy', question: 'A customer has low electric bills, a solid roof, and good insulation, but frequently loses power during storms and worries about food spoilage. What problem category is most relevant?', choices: ['A roof problem', 'A building envelope problem', 'An energy cost problem', 'A resilience/backup power consideration, potentially involving battery storage'], answerIndex: 3, explain: 'Frequent outages and concern about backup power point toward evaluating battery storage for resilience, not cost, envelope, or roofing issues.' },
  { id: 'ea-042', category: 'Whole-Home Energy', question: 'What distinguishes an "energy problem" from an "energy cost problem" in whole-home diagnosis?', choices: ['There is no meaningful distinction between the two', 'An energy problem may involve usage patterns or fuel type, while a cost problem centers specifically on the dollar amount being paid regardless of usage efficiency', 'Energy problems only apply to solar customers', 'Cost problems only apply to HVAC customers'], answerIndex: 1, explain: 'While related, usage/fuel-type issues (energy problem) and the dollar impact of rates or fuel cost (cost problem) can have different root causes and solutions.' },
  { id: 'ea-043', category: 'Whole-Home Energy', question: 'A prospective customer asks, "Should I just get solar panels since that\'s what everyone talks about?" What is the best whole-home-thinking response?', choices: ['Explain that the right solution depends on their specific situation, and offer to walk through a short discovery conversation first', 'Agree and schedule a solar-only installation immediately', 'Tell them solar is never a good idea without further explanation', 'Redirect them only to HVAC without asking any questions'], answerIndex: 0, explain: 'The consultative approach means diagnosing the customer\'s specific situation rather than defaulting to whichever product is most popular.' },
  { id: 'ea-044', category: 'Whole-Home Energy', question: 'Which best describes how whole-home energy thinking changes the advisor\'s first move with a new customer?', choices: ['The first move is always presenting the solar proposal', 'The first move is always presenting financing options', 'The first move is discovery and diagnosis, not a product pitch', 'The first move is always scheduling an installation crew'], answerIndex: 2, explain: 'Whole-home thinking reorients the sales process to start with diagnosis rather than a predetermined product pitch.' },

  // ===================== SOLUTION MAPPING (14, heavily scenario-based) =====================
  { id: 'ea-045', category: 'Solution Mapping', question: 'Customer profile: high electric bill, old oil furnace, no central AC, roof in good condition. Which solutions deserve further evaluation?', choices: ['Only a new oil furnace should be evaluated', 'Both heat pump and solar are worth evaluating; the roof is not a blocker', 'Only roof replacement should be evaluated', 'Nothing further is needed since the roof is fine'], answerIndex: 1, explain: 'With a good roof and an inefficient oil/no-AC setup, both a heat pump (addressing heating and cooling) and solar (addressing electric cost) are worth evaluating together.' },
  { id: 'ea-046', category: 'Solution Mapping', question: 'Customer profile: new HVAC system already installed, old roof, high electricity usage, planning to buy an EV. What should the advisor prioritize?', choices: ['Immediately replace the recently installed HVAC system to pair better with solar', 'Sell solar immediately without checking the roof', 'Ignore the EV plans since they are not confirmed yet', 'Roof inspection before solar, and electrical capacity check before EV charger and solar; do not re-sell HVAC'], answerIndex: 3, explain: 'The roof needs inspection before solar, electrical capacity needs checking before adding EV and solar loads, and the recently installed HVAC should not be re-sold.' },
  { id: 'ea-047', category: 'Solution Mapping', question: 'Customer profile: solar already installed, poor insulation, cold bedrooms, oil heating. What is the priority?', choices: ['Adding more solar panels to cover the cold bedrooms', 'Recommending a battery to fix the cold bedrooms', 'Insulation/weatherization and heat pump zoning, not additional solar', 'Doing nothing since solar is already installed'], answerIndex: 2, explain: 'The core issues here are envelope and heating comfort, not solar capacity — weatherization and a properly zoned heat pump address the actual complaint.' },
  { id: 'ea-048', category: 'Solution Mapping', question: 'Customer profile: recently replaced roof, all-electric baseboard heat, high electric bills, considering solar. Which solutions deserve further evaluation?', choices: ['Both solar (good roof, high usage) and heat pump conversion (inefficient baseboard heat) are worth evaluating', 'Only a roof inspection, since the roof was just replaced', 'Nothing, since baseboard heat cannot be replaced', 'Only battery storage, ignoring the heating inefficiency'], answerIndex: 0, explain: 'A new roof clears the way for solar, and inefficient baseboard electric heat is a strong candidate for heat pump conversion to reduce the underlying cost driver.' },
  { id: 'ea-049', category: 'Solution Mapping', question: 'Customer profile: 15-year-old roof showing granule loss and a few curling shingles, strong interest in solar, moderate electric bill. What should the advisor recommend as the next step?', choices: ['Immediate solar installation since interest is high', 'Skip roofing concerns since the customer wants solar', 'A professional roof inspection before finalizing any solar design', 'Recommend a battery instead of addressing the roof'], answerIndex: 2, explain: 'Visible wear signs (granule loss, curling shingles) warrant a professional inspection before committing to a solar installation on that roof.' },
  { id: 'ea-050', category: 'Solution Mapping', question: 'Customer profile: gas heating with central AC already in place, well-insulated home, no major comfort complaints, moderate electric bill, no major future plans. What is the appropriate advisor posture?', choices: ['Acknowledge that no service may need immediate further evaluation, and offer to revisit if their situation changes', 'Aggressively pitch solar, HVAC, and battery in the same visit', 'Insist there must be a problem somewhere and keep probing until they buy something', 'Recommend a full HVAC replacement despite no complaints'], answerIndex: 0, explain: 'Consultative advising means recognizing when a customer\'s situation does not currently warrant a service change, rather than manufacturing a sale.' },
  { id: 'ea-051', category: 'Solution Mapping', question: 'Customer profile: 200-amp panel already at near-full capacity, wants EV charger, heat pump, and solar with battery all at once. What should the advisor flag first?', choices: ['Proceeding with all installations simultaneously without any electrical review', 'Recommending against EV ownership entirely', 'Assuming the panel is fine because it is 200 amps', 'A qualified electrical review to determine whether the panel/service can support the combined new loads'], answerIndex: 3, explain: 'Even a 200-amp panel can be insufficient once combined new loads (EV, heat pump, solar, battery) are added — a qualified electrician should verify capacity first.' },
  { id: 'ea-052', category: 'Solution Mapping', question: 'Customer profile: drafty windows, visible daylight under exterior doors, aging oil furnace, no immediate solar interest. What deserves further evaluation first?', choices: ['Solar installation despite no expressed interest', 'Air sealing/weatherization assessment before finalizing any HVAC replacement sizing', 'Battery storage as the first priority', 'Ignore the drafts since the customer did not ask about them'], answerIndex: 1, explain: 'Significant air leakage should be addressed or at least assessed before finalizing HVAC sizing, since it changes the real heating load.' },
  { id: 'ea-053', category: 'Solution Mapping', question: 'Customer profile: recently finished an attic insulation upgrade, oil heat, no AC, considering a heat pump. What is the appropriate next evaluation step?', choices: ['Proceed with heat pump load calculation, since the envelope work is already addressed', 'Insist on another insulation upgrade before considering HVAC', 'Recommend solar first regardless of heating needs', 'Tell the customer heat pumps are not compatible with oil-heated homes'], answerIndex: 0, explain: 'With envelope improvements already done, a heat pump load calculation can proceed as a logical next step to address heating and cooling.' },
  { id: 'ea-054', category: 'Solution Mapping', question: 'Customer profile: landlord evaluating a rental property with a 25-year-old furnace, tenants complain about cold rooms, landlord plans to sell within 2 years. Which factor most affects the recommendation?', choices: ['The tenant\'s personal preferences for thermostat brand', 'The property\'s roof color', 'The short ownership horizon, which affects whether long-payback investments like solar make sense versus a straightforward equipment fix', 'The landlord\'s favorite home improvement show'], answerIndex: 2, explain: 'A short planned ownership window changes which investments make financial sense — long-payback options like solar are less suited than a direct comfort/equipment fix.' },
  { id: 'ea-055', category: 'Solution Mapping', question: 'Customer profile: home has a whole-house generator already, no interest in backup power, but is interested in maximizing solar self-consumption. What should the advisor evaluate?', choices: ['Automatically recommend battery storage sized for whole-home backup since they already understand backup power', 'Whether a battery makes sense for self-consumption/demand management rather than backup, based on the customer\'s actual goal', 'Skip discussing battery storage entirely since they have a generator', 'Assume backup power is still the primary goal'], answerIndex: 1, explain: 'Since the stated goal is self-consumption, not backup, the battery conversation should focus on demand management value rather than duplicating existing backup capability.' },
  { id: 'ea-056', category: 'Solution Mapping', question: 'Customer profile: recently purchased an EV, home has an older 100-amp panel, currently gas heating with no plans to change it, moderate electric bill. What is the most relevant next step?', choices: ['Immediately propose a full HVAC and solar package', 'Ignore the EV charging need since gas heating is unrelated', 'Recommend battery storage as the top priority', 'Evaluate electrical capacity for EV charging needs; HVAC and solar are not urgent priorities given no stated plans or major cost issue'], answerIndex: 3, explain: 'The customer\'s actual immediate need is EV charging capacity; HVAC and solar are not indicated by their stated situation.' },
  { id: 'ea-057', category: 'Solution Mapping', question: 'Customer profile: 2,200 sq ft Massachusetts home, oil heating, no central AC, $350 average electric bill, 18-year-old roof, poor attic insulation, planning an EV purchase. What is the correct advisor approach?', choices: ['Sell a full solar, HVAC, and battery package on the first visit without further evaluation', 'Only discuss solar since that is the highest-margin product', 'Tell the customer nothing can be done until the roof is replaced', 'Identify roof, insulation, and electrical capacity as items needing specialist review, and sequence solutions rather than closing everything at once'], answerIndex: 3, explain: 'This capstone scenario requires diagnosing roof age, insulation, and electrical capacity issues, referring to specialists as needed, and proposing a phased next-step plan rather than a one-visit close.' },
  { id: 'ea-058', category: 'Solution Mapping', question: 'Customer profile: recently completed a whole-home electrical panel upgrade (from 100A to 200A) for unrelated reasons, now exploring solar and battery. What does this favorably affect?', choices: ['It guarantees the home needs no further review of any kind', 'It likely reduces or removes electrical capacity concerns for solar and battery addition, though it should still be confirmed against the proposed system design', 'It has no bearing on the solar/battery conversation', 'It means an EV charger and heat pump could never be added later'], answerIndex: 1, explain: 'A recent panel upgrade is a positive factor, but capacity should still be confirmed against the specific proposed solar/battery design rather than assumed unlimited.' },

  // ===================== COMPLIANCE (10) =====================
  { id: 'ea-059', category: 'Compliance', question: 'Which of these four advisor statements is compliant and appropriately non-overpromising? A) "Your bill will drop to zero guaranteed." B) "Based on your usage data, solar could significantly offset your bill, though the exact amount depends on production and rate factors." C) "You\'ll get the full 30% rebate for sure." D) "I can tell your roof is fine without an inspection."', choices: ['B — it references data while avoiding a guaranteed outcome', 'A — it gives the customer a clear guarantee', 'C — it commits to a specific rebate amount', 'D — it skips professional roof review'], answerIndex: 0, explain: 'Statement B ties the estimate to real data while avoiding an absolute guarantee, which matches compliant, non-overpromising advising.' },
  { id: 'ea-060', category: 'Compliance', question: 'A customer asks the advisor to confirm their roof can support solar panels without any inspection. What is the compliant response?', choices: ['Confirm the roof is fine based on a visual glance from the driveway', 'Tell the customer roofing condition never matters for solar', 'Explain that a professional roof inspection is needed before making that determination', 'Guarantee the roof will last as long as the panels'], answerIndex: 2, explain: 'Advisors do not make roofing engineering determinations personally — a qualified inspection is required before confirming roof suitability.' },
  { id: 'ea-061', category: 'Compliance', question: 'Which statement about battery backup is compliant?', choices: ['"The battery will power your entire home indefinitely during any outage."', '"Battery storage can provide backup for select loads when properly designed and configured for your home."', '"All batteries automatically back up every circuit in the house."', '"You will never lose power again once the battery is installed."'], answerIndex: 1, explain: 'Backup scope depends on system design and configuration; advisors should not promise full or indefinite backup without a confirmed design.' },
  { id: 'ea-062', category: 'Compliance', question: 'A customer asks how large their electrical service upgrade needs to be for a heat pump, EV charger, and solar combined. What is the compliant response?', choices: ['Give an exact amperage number without any electrician review', 'Tell the customer their panel is definitely sufficient without checking', 'Avoid the topic since it is not the advisor\'s job to mention it at all', 'Explain that a qualified electrician should evaluate the panel and service capacity for the combined loads'], answerIndex: 3, explain: 'Electrical capacity determinations require a qualified electrician\'s review — the advisor\'s role is to flag the need, not make the engineering call.' },
  { id: 'ea-063', category: 'Compliance', question: 'Which of these four statements about HVAC sizing is compliant? A) "We\'ll just install what the last company had." B) "Bigger is always better, so we\'ll go up a size." C) "We\'ll perform a load calculation to determine the right size for your home." D) "Sizing doesn\'t really matter for heat pumps."', choices: ['A — it copies a previous, unverified installation', 'B — it defaults to oversizing', 'C — it references a proper load calculation', 'D — it dismisses sizing accuracy entirely'], answerIndex: 2, explain: 'Statement C is the only one grounded in a verified sizing method (Manual J or equivalent), avoiding both oversizing and undersizing risk.' },
  { id: 'ea-064', category: 'Compliance', question: 'A customer asks for a specific dollar figure they will save annually with solar before any usage analysis has been done. What should the advisor do?', choices: ['Explain that estimates require analyzing their usage data first and avoid committing to an unverified number', 'Give a rounded estimate on the spot without any data', 'Guarantee a savings figure to close the deal faster', 'Tell the customer savings estimates are illegal to discuss'], answerIndex: 0, explain: 'Savings estimates should be grounded in actual usage analysis; advisors must never guarantee a specific dollar savings figure, especially without data.' },
  { id: 'ea-065', category: 'Compliance', question: 'Which of these installation timeline statements is compliant? A) "Installation always happens within one week, guaranteed." B) "Timelines depend on permitting, utility approval, and scheduling, and we\'ll keep you updated." C) "We never have delays." D) "It will definitely be done before winter no matter what."', choices: ['A — it guarantees an unrealistic fixed timeline', 'C — it denies the possibility of delays', 'B — it acknowledges real variables affecting timeline', 'D — it makes an unconditional seasonal promise'], answerIndex: 2, explain: 'Timelines depend on permitting and utility processes outside the company\'s full control, so compliant language avoids absolute promises.' },
  { id: 'ea-066', category: 'Compliance', question: 'A customer asks the advisor to confirm their exact tax credit eligibility on the spot. What is the appropriate response?', choices: ['Reference general program information and recommend the customer confirm details with a tax professional or official source', 'Confirm eligibility and the exact amount immediately', 'Tell the customer tax credits do not apply to their situation without checking', 'Avoid the topic of incentives altogether'], answerIndex: 0, explain: 'Tax credit eligibility and amounts should not be guaranteed by the advisor without verification — customers should be directed to confirm with a qualified source.' },
  { id: 'ea-067', category: 'Compliance', question: 'Which of these four statements about a battery\'s backup scope is most compliant? A) "It will back up your whole house no matter what you add later." B) "Backup scope depends on the specific design — we\'ll confirm which circuits are covered once the system is designed." C) "All batteries back up everything by default." D) "Backup coverage never needs to be discussed with the customer."', choices: ['B — it ties backup scope to a confirmed system design', 'C — it assumes a universal default that is not accurate', 'D — it avoids the topic entirely, leaving the customer uninformed', 'A — it promises unconditional whole-home backup'], answerIndex: 3, explain: 'Backup scope varies by system design, so the compliant answer ties any specific claim to a confirmed design rather than a blanket promise.' },
  { id: 'ea-068', category: 'Compliance', question: 'A customer\'s insulation is clearly deficient, and the advisor is not a certified weatherization assessor. What is the compliant next step?', choices: ['Give a specific R-value recommendation without any assessment', 'Recommend a qualified insulation/weatherization assessment rather than personally diagnosing the exact insulation deficiency', 'Ignore the insulation issue since it is outside the advisor\'s product line', 'Tell the customer insulation never affects HVAC performance'], answerIndex: 1, explain: 'Just as with roofing and electrical, insulation specifics should be referred to a qualified assessor rather than determined personally by the advisor.' },

  // ===================== UTILITY BILLS (8) =====================
  { id: 'ea-069', category: 'Utility Bills', question: 'Why should an advisor request 12 months of utility bills rather than just the most recent one?', choices: ['A full year captures seasonal usage variation, which affects sizing accuracy', 'One month is always sufficient because usage never varies', 'Utility companies require 12 months to issue a bill', 'It is only needed for financing, not for sizing'], answerIndex: 0, explain: 'Seasonal variation (summer cooling, winter heating) means a single month\'s bill does not represent the home\'s full annual usage pattern.' },
  { id: 'ea-070', category: 'Utility Bills', question: 'A customer\'s bill shows a "delivery charge" and a "supply charge" as separate line items. What should the advisor understand about this?', choices: ['Only the supply charge exists; delivery charges are a billing error', 'Solar eliminates both charges completely in every case', 'Both components make up the total cost of electricity and may be affected differently by solar production credits', 'These charges are identical and interchangeable'], answerIndex: 2, explain: 'Utility bills often separate supply and delivery costs, and understanding both helps set accurate expectations about how solar credits may or may not offset each component.' },
  { id: 'ea-071', category: 'Utility Bills', question: 'A customer\'s bill shows unusually high usage in July and August compared to the rest of the year. What does this most likely indicate?', choices: ['A billing error that should be reported immediately without investigation', 'Air conditioning or cooling load driving summer electricity consumption', 'Solar panels malfunctioning', 'Heating system usage, since summer heating is common in New England'], answerIndex: 1, explain: 'Summer usage spikes are typically driven by air conditioning and cooling loads, which is useful context for HVAC and solar sizing conversations.' },
  { id: 'ea-072', category: 'Utility Bills', question: 'Why might an advisor ask whether the customer is on a fixed or variable electricity rate plan?', choices: ['Rate plans have no effect on solar or cost estimates', 'This is only relevant for gas customers, not electric', 'Fixed and variable rates always produce identical bills', 'Rate structure affects how usage translates to cost and how solar savings should be estimated'], answerIndex: 3, explain: 'Rate structure changes how kWh usage translates into dollar cost, which matters for accurately framing potential solar savings.' },
  { id: 'ea-073', category: 'Utility Bills', question: 'A customer asks why their bill increased significantly after they started charging an EV at home. What is the most accurate explanation?', choices: ['EV charging has no meaningful effect on electric bills', 'The increase must be a utility billing error', 'EVs only draw power from a separate, unbilled source', 'EV charging adds substantial electricity load, which increases overall consumption and cost'], answerIndex: 3, explain: 'EV charging is a significant added electrical load and commonly increases household electricity consumption and cost.' },
  { id: 'ea-074', category: 'Utility Bills', question: 'What is the value of reviewing a customer\'s bill for "net metering credits" if they already have solar installed elsewhere on a previous system?', choices: ['Net metering credits are irrelevant to any future recommendations', 'It shows how much of their solar production is being credited versus consumed directly, informing future sizing or battery conversations', 'It only matters if the customer is canceling their solar service', 'Net metering credits always equal the full retail rate everywhere'], answerIndex: 1, explain: 'Understanding how existing solar credits appear on the bill helps inform whether additional solar or a battery might add further value.' },
  { id: 'ea-075', category: 'Utility Bills', question: 'A customer\'s utility bill shows a demand charge in addition to usage charges. Why is this relevant to an advisor?', choices: ['Demand charges are based on peak usage and may be influenced by battery storage or load management strategies', 'Demand charges never appear on residential bills and should be disregarded', 'Demand charges are unrelated to any Nexis service', 'Demand charges only apply to solar customers'], answerIndex: 0, explain: 'Demand charges reflect peak power draw and can potentially be managed with battery storage or usage timing, making them relevant to a whole-home conversation.' },
  { id: 'ea-076', category: 'Utility Bills', question: 'Why is it important to distinguish between a customer\'s "average monthly bill" and their "highest monthly bill" during discovery?', choices: ['Only the average bill matters; peak months are irrelevant', 'The highest bill is always a billing mistake', 'The highest bill often reveals peak seasonal loads (e.g., summer AC or winter heating) that the average alone would obscure', 'These two figures are always identical'], answerIndex: 2, explain: 'Peak-month bills reveal seasonal extremes in usage that an average figure can mask, which matters for accurate sizing and diagnosis.' },

  // ===================== CUSTOMER SCENARIOS (14, heavily scenario-based) =====================
  { id: 'ea-077', category: 'Customer Scenarios', question: 'A customer calls about a $400 monthly electric bill with electric baseboard heat, a well-insulated home, and no plans to move. They want to reduce costs long-term. What should the advisor evaluate first?', choices: ['Only a roof inspection, since roofing is unrelated to the complaint', 'Heat pump conversion to replace inefficient baseboard heat, alongside a solar evaluation given the high usage', 'Immediately close a battery storage sale', 'Nothing, since the home is already well-insulated'], answerIndex: 1, explain: 'Baseboard electric heat is highly inefficient; converting to a heat pump directly addresses the cost driver, and solar is worth evaluating given the high usage.' },
  { id: 'ea-078', category: 'Customer Scenarios', question: 'A young couple just bought their first home with a 30-year-old furnace, no AC, and a roof that was replaced 3 years ago. They want to plan improvements over the next several years but have a limited budget right now. What is the best advisor approach?', choices: ['Insist they do everything at once regardless of budget', 'Recommend solar first since the roof is new, ignoring the aging furnace', 'Tell them to wait until the furnace completely fails before doing anything', 'Build a phased energy roadmap starting with the most urgent/impactful item (likely HVAC given furnace age) and sequence other items over time'], answerIndex: 3, explain: 'A phased roadmap matches limited budgets and prioritizes the most pressing need (an aging furnace) while sequencing other upgrades over time.' },
  { id: 'ea-079', category: 'Customer Scenarios', question: 'A customer recently retired and is on a fixed income. They are interested in solar but worried about upfront costs and long payback periods. What should the advisor emphasize?', choices: ['Pressure them to sign quickly before they can reconsider', 'Avoid discussing costs since it might discourage them', 'Explore financing options and realistic payback timelines transparently, without overselling or pressuring a decision', 'Guarantee the system will pay for itself within one year regardless of actual data'], answerIndex: 2, explain: 'For a budget-sensitive customer, transparent discussion of financing and realistic payback timelines respects their situation without pressure or overpromising.' },
  { id: 'ea-080', category: 'Customer Scenarios', question: 'A customer\'s home has a propane furnace, propane is unusually expensive in their area, and they have excellent roof condition and insulation. They ask, "What would you actually recommend for a home like mine?" What is the best next step?', choices: ['Recommend evaluating a heat pump conversion given the expensive fuel source, with solar also worth exploring given favorable roof conditions', 'Recommend keeping the propane furnace exactly as-is', 'Recommend only a battery with no other changes', 'Avoid giving any recommendation and end the conversation'], answerIndex: 0, explain: 'Expensive propane heating combined with good roof/envelope conditions makes both heat pump conversion and solar strong candidates for further evaluation.' },
  { id: 'ea-081', category: 'Customer Scenarios', question: 'A customer with existing solar and a heat pump says their electric bills increased noticeably after the heat pump was installed by another contractor. They ask if their solar system is now "too small." What should the advisor do?', choices: ['Automatically tell them the existing solar system is fine without checking data', 'Blame the heat pump installer without investigating usage', 'Review actual pre- and post-electrification usage data to determine if the existing solar system is undersized relative to the new load', 'Immediately recommend doubling their solar system size without analysis'], answerIndex: 2, explain: 'Electrification (adding a heat pump) can increase electric usage; the advisor should verify actual usage data before concluding whether the solar system needs to be resized.' },
  { id: 'ea-082', category: 'Customer Scenarios', question: 'A customer wants "the best of everything" — solar, battery, heat pump, and an EV charger — installed as fast as possible, without wanting to discuss details. What is the appropriate advisor response?', choices: ['Explain that a short discovery and sequencing conversation will help ensure the systems work well together and avoid costly rework', 'Immediately schedule all four installations without any discovery', 'Refuse to work with the customer because they want too much', 'Randomly pick an order of installation without any diagnostic basis'], answerIndex: 0, explain: 'Even an eager customer benefits from discovery and sequencing to avoid costly mistakes like undersized electrical capacity or mismatched system design.' },
  { id: 'ea-083', category: 'Customer Scenarios', question: 'A customer says their neighbor got solar and now "barely pays anything," and they want the same guarantee. How should the advisor respond?', choices: ['Promise an identical outcome regardless of differences between homes', 'Tell the customer their neighbor is lying', 'Avoid addressing the comparison at all', 'Explain that results vary by home, usage, and system design, and offer to run their own personalized analysis'], answerIndex: 3, explain: 'Every home\'s usage, roof, and system design differ, so results cannot be guaranteed to match a neighbor\'s — a personalized analysis is the appropriate path.' },
  { id: 'ea-084', category: 'Customer Scenarios', question: 'A customer is renovating their basement into a home office and mentions they\'ll need better temperature control down there, on top of asking about solar. What should the advisor do?', choices: ['Ignore the basement comment since it is unrelated to solar', 'Address both topics: evaluate comfort/zoning solutions for the basement and continue the solar discovery separately', 'Recommend solar as the fix for the basement temperature issue', 'Tell the customer basement comfort problems cannot be solved'], answerIndex: 1, explain: 'Comfort issues in a renovated space are a separate diagnostic thread from solar and should be addressed with appropriate zoning/comfort solutions.' },
  { id: 'ea-085', category: 'Customer Scenarios', question: 'A customer explains that their biggest concern is resale value, not personal comfort or bill savings, before selling their home in 18 months. What should shape the advisor\'s recommendation?', choices: ['Focus on improvements with strong resale appeal and short payback relevance given the 18-month timeline', 'Recommend a 25-year solar loan regardless of the short timeline', 'Ignore the stated timeline and proceed with standard recommendations', 'Refuse to help since the timeline is too short for any service'], answerIndex: 0, explain: 'A near-term sale changes which investments make sense — the advisor should weigh resale value and shorter-payback options over long-horizon financing.' },
  { id: 'ea-086', category: 'Customer Scenarios', question: 'A customer\'s home has an oil tank that is due for replacement soon, alongside interest in going all-electric to avoid future oil costs. What should the advisor evaluate?', choices: ['Recommend replacing the oil tank and ignoring their stated interest in going electric', 'Recommend only a battery with no heating discussion', 'Heat pump conversion as an alternative to replacing the oil system, factoring in the customer\'s stated preference to move away from oil', 'Tell the customer electrification is not possible in oil-heated homes'], answerIndex: 2, explain: 'Given the customer\'s stated goal to move away from oil and an oil tank nearing replacement, heat pump conversion aligns directly with their objective.' },
  { id: 'ea-087', category: 'Customer Scenarios', question: 'A customer is hearing-impaired and communicates primarily via text/email. They ask detailed technical questions about system design in writing. What is the appropriate advisor approach?', choices: ['Insist on a phone call as the only acceptable communication method', 'Provide clear, accurate written answers and documentation, adapting communication to their preferred method', 'Give vague answers since detailed technical responses are hard to write', 'Ignore their written questions until they call'], answerIndex: 1, explain: 'Adapting to the customer\'s preferred and accessible communication method while still providing accurate, complete information is the appropriate consultative approach.' },
  { id: 'ea-088', category: 'Customer Scenarios', question: 'A customer mentions they are considering a competitor\'s quote that promises a much shorter payback period than what Nexis has estimated. What is the best advisor response?', choices: ['Immediately match or beat the competitor\'s number without reviewing assumptions', 'Disparage the competitor without addressing the substance of the claim', 'Refuse to discuss the competing quote at all', 'Review the assumptions behind both estimates transparently, without disparaging the competitor or inflating Nexis\'s own numbers'], answerIndex: 3, explain: 'A transparent, assumption-based comparison respects the customer\'s decision-making without resorting to inflated claims or unprofessional comparisons.' },
  { id: 'ea-089', category: 'Customer Scenarios', question: 'A multi-generational household has very high hot water and heating demand, older HVAC equipment, and is interested in reducing costs but unsure where to start. What should the advisor do?', choices: ['Recommend the same standard package regardless of their unique usage patterns', 'Tell them nothing can be done because the household is too large', 'Skip discovery since the family already knows they need "something"', 'Conduct thorough discovery on usage patterns and equipment age before recommending a phased set of solutions'], answerIndex: 3, explain: 'Higher and more complex usage patterns in a multi-generational household make thorough discovery especially important before proposing solutions.' },
  { id: 'ea-090', category: 'Customer Scenarios', question: 'A customer with a very tight budget asks the advisor to recommend just one single improvement that would make the biggest difference in comfort and cost. Given an old, leaky attic and an aging but functional furnace, what is the most defensible single recommendation?', choices: ['A brand-new solar system, since it is the most visible upgrade', 'Attic insulation/air sealing, since it is often a high-impact, comparatively lower-cost improvement that also benefits future HVAC performance', 'A luxury smart thermostat with no envelope work', 'A whole-home battery system'], answerIndex: 1, explain: 'For budget-constrained customers, envelope improvements like attic insulation/air sealing often deliver strong comfort and cost impact at comparatively lower cost, and improve the effectiveness of future HVAC work.' },

  // ===================== CROSS-SELLING LOGIC (12) =====================
  { id: 'ea-091', category: 'Cross-Selling Logic', question: 'Why does adding a heat pump potentially require revisiting a previously finalized solar sizing estimate?', choices: ['Electrification increases electricity consumption, which changes the usage baseline solar sizing was built on', 'Heat pumps have no effect on electricity usage', 'Solar sizing is never affected by heating system changes', 'Heat pumps reduce electricity usage in every case'], answerIndex: 0, explain: 'Since heat pumps shift heating load onto electricity, a solar system sized before knowing about planned electrification may end up undersized.' },
  { id: 'ea-092', category: 'Cross-Selling Logic', question: 'What is the risk of sizing a solar system without first asking about a customer\'s electrification plans (EV, heat pump)?', choices: ['There is no risk since solar sizing is unrelated to electrification', 'The system will always be automatically oversized as a safety margin', 'The system may be undersized once future electric loads are added', 'Electrification plans only matter for HVAC financing, not solar sizing'], answerIndex: 2, explain: 'Failing to account for anticipated electrification can lead directly to an undersized solar system relative to future actual usage.' },
  { id: 'ea-093', category: 'Cross-Selling Logic', question: 'A customer wants solar and is currently heating with oil but is undecided about future heat pump plans. What should the advisor do regarding sizing?', choices: ['Size the system strictly for current usage and never mention future electrification', 'Discuss the possibility of future electrification and how it could affect sizing, even if the decision is not yet final', 'Assume the customer will never electrify and ignore the topic', 'Refuse to provide a solar estimate until the heat pump decision is finalized'], answerIndex: 1, explain: 'Even with an undecided heat pump plan, the advisor should raise the topic so the customer can make an informed sizing decision — including options like designing for future expansion.' },
  { id: 'ea-094', category: 'Cross-Selling Logic', question: 'Why should HVAC zone/load assumptions account for building envelope conditions rather than relying on square footage alone?', choices: ['Square footage is the only variable that matters for load calculations', 'Envelope conditions never affect heat pump sizing', 'Zone assumptions are unrelated to insulation quality', 'Poor insulation or air sealing changes actual heat loss/gain, which square footage alone does not capture'], answerIndex: 3, explain: 'Two homes with identical square footage can have very different heating/cooling loads depending on insulation and air sealing quality.' },
  { id: 'ea-095', category: 'Cross-Selling Logic', question: 'A customer wants both an EV charger and a heat pump installed in the same project. Why is a combined electrical capacity review important, rather than reviewing each separately?', choices: ['Each device draws power at completely different times, so combined review is unnecessary', 'EV chargers and heat pumps never draw power simultaneously', 'The combined load of both additions together may exceed what a review of either one alone would suggest is safe', 'Electrical review is only needed if a customer requests it explicitly'], answerIndex: 2, explain: 'Reviewing loads individually can miss capacity issues that only appear when combined loads are considered together on the same panel/service.' },
  { id: 'ea-096', category: 'Cross-Selling Logic', question: 'A customer\'s roof is in questionable condition and they are also interested in a heat pump. Should the roof condition affect the HVAC recommendation timeline?', choices: ['Generally no — roof condition primarily affects solar timing, not standalone HVAC installation, though outdoor unit placement should still be considered', 'Yes — HVAC installation must always wait for a full roof replacement first', 'Roof condition makes heat pumps impossible to install under any circumstance', 'HVAC and roofing are always installed simultaneously regardless of condition'], answerIndex: 0, explain: 'Roof condition is most critical for solar timing; a standalone HVAC (especially ductless) project is generally not blocked by roof condition, though placement details should still be considered.' },
  { id: 'ea-097', category: 'Cross-Selling Logic', question: 'Why is it important to check electrical capacity before finalizing a battery storage recommendation alongside solar?', choices: ['Battery storage never interacts with a home\'s electrical panel', 'Electrical capacity is only relevant for EV chargers, not batteries', 'Battery systems add to the total electrical load/interconnection requirements that a panel and service must support', 'Batteries always reduce panel capacity requirements automatically'], answerIndex: 2, explain: 'Batteries integrate with the home\'s electrical system and interconnection, so capacity and configuration should be verified alongside solar design.' },
  { id: 'ea-098', category: 'Cross-Selling Logic', question: 'A customer had oil heat and is switching to a heat pump, dropping oil consumption to near zero. How should this affect the customer\'s overall energy cost conversation?', choices: ['The advisor should discuss the net effect: reduced oil spend but increased electric usage, and how solar could offset the new electric load', 'The advisor should only discuss electric costs and ignore the oil savings entirely', 'The advisor should assume total costs will stay exactly the same', 'The advisor should assume oil savings alone make solar unnecessary'], answerIndex: 0, explain: 'A complete picture requires weighing reduced fuel oil spend against increased electric usage, and considering solar as a way to offset the new electric load.' },
  { id: 'ea-099', category: 'Cross-Selling Logic', question: 'What is the danger of treating solar, HVAC, insulation, and electrical work as four completely separate, unrelated sales conversations?', choices: ['There is no danger; each area is entirely independent of the others', 'Separating the conversations always produces more accurate results', 'Only electrical and solar interact; HVAC and insulation are unrelated', 'Decisions made in one area (e.g., HVAC sizing) can be based on inaccurate assumptions if envelope or electrical factors are not considered together'], answerIndex: 3, explain: 'These systems interact — envelope conditions affect HVAC sizing, electrification affects solar sizing, and combined loads affect electrical capacity — so a siloed approach risks costly mistakes.' },
  { id: 'ea-100', category: 'Cross-Selling Logic', question: 'A customer already has a battery and is now adding solar for the first time. What cross-service consideration is most relevant?', choices: ['Assuming any solar system will automatically work with any existing battery with no verification', 'Verifying the existing battery\'s compatibility and configuration with the new solar system design', 'Ignoring the existing battery during solar design entirely', 'Requiring the customer to replace the battery regardless of compatibility'], answerIndex: 1, explain: 'Existing equipment should be verified for compatibility with new solar design rather than assumed to integrate automatically.' },
  { id: 'ea-101', category: 'Cross-Selling Logic', question: 'Why might an advisor recommend a smaller initial HVAC/solar project with a planned future expansion path, rather than one large combined project, for some customers?', choices: ['It can match the customer\'s budget and confirmed near-term needs while leaving room for verified future additions like EV charging', 'Phased projects are always more expensive with no benefit', 'Future expansion is never technically possible once a system is installed', 'Customers should always be pushed into the largest possible project regardless of budget'], answerIndex: 0, explain: 'A phased approach can match budget and current confirmed needs while still allowing well-planned future additions, which fits the consultative, non-pressure approach.' },
  { id: 'ea-102', category: 'Cross-Selling Logic', question: 'A customer\'s attic insulation is currently being addressed by a separate contractor before the Nexis HVAC installation. Why does this timing matter?', choices: ['Insulation work never affects HVAC load calculations regardless of timing', 'HVAC installation should always happen before any insulation work', 'Completing insulation work first gives a more accurate envelope condition for final HVAC load calculations', 'The order of these two projects has no bearing on outcomes'], answerIndex: 2, explain: 'Since insulation changes heat-loss assumptions, completing it before finalizing HVAC load calculations produces more accurate sizing.' },

  // ===================== ENERGY PLANNING (12) =====================
  { id: 'ea-103', category: 'Energy Planning', question: 'What is the purpose of building a phased "Home Energy Roadmap" for a customer?', choices: ['To force the customer to buy every service in a single transaction', 'To sequence solutions in a logical order tailored to the customer\'s specific situation and priorities', 'To guarantee a fixed price regardless of scope changes', 'To replace the need for any discovery process'], answerIndex: 1, explain: 'A phased roadmap sequences recommendations logically based on the customer\'s specific diagnosis, rather than pushing an all-at-once sale.' },
  { id: 'ea-104', category: 'Energy Planning', question: 'For a customer with poor attic insulation who is also considering a heat pump, what is the correct sequencing in a typical roadmap?', choices: ['Install the heat pump first, then never revisit insulation', 'Insulation and HVAC sizing are always independent of sequencing', 'Install solar first, regardless of insulation or HVAC status', 'Weatherize/insulate before finalizing HVAC sizing'], answerIndex: 3, explain: 'Since envelope conditions affect heat-loss assumptions, weatherization should generally come before or alongside finalizing HVAC sizing.' },
  { id: 'ea-105', category: 'Energy Planning', question: 'For a customer with an old roof who is also interested in solar, what is the correct sequencing in a typical roadmap?', choices: ['Install solar first and inspect the roof afterward', 'Roof condition never needs to be addressed before solar', 'Skip roof evaluation since panels protect the roof regardless of condition', 'Inspect/address the roof before finalizing solar installation'], answerIndex: 3, explain: 'Roof condition should be resolved before solar to avoid the cost and disruption of removing and reinstalling panels for a later roof repair.' },
  { id: 'ea-106', category: 'Energy Planning', question: 'A customer has no near-term EV plans and a limited budget. Should an EV-ready electrical panel upgrade be prioritized first in their roadmap?', choices: ['Yes — it should always be the very first phase regardless of stated plans', 'No — it should not be prioritized first since there is no near-term need; it can be revisited if plans change', 'Yes — EV readiness is required before any other electrification work', 'It is irrelevant to roadmap planning either way'], answerIndex: 1, explain: 'Without near-term EV plans, prioritizing an EV-ready panel upgrade first would not match the customer\'s actual near-term needs or budget.' },
  { id: 'ea-107', category: 'Energy Planning', question: 'Which sequence best reflects a typical example roadmap taught in this curriculum: Phase 1 through Phase 4?', choices: ['Phase 1: Energy Assessment + Weatherization; Phase 2: Heat Pump; Phase 3: Solar; Phase 4: Battery/EV Charger', 'Phase 1: Battery/EV Charger; Phase 2: Solar; Phase 3: Heat Pump; Phase 4: Energy Assessment', 'Phase 1: Solar; Phase 2: Battery; Phase 3: Energy Assessment; Phase 4: Heat Pump', 'All four phases must always happen simultaneously with no sequencing'], answerIndex: 0, explain: 'The example roadmap starts with assessment/weatherization (foundational diagnosis and envelope), then heat pump, then solar, then battery/EV — though the correct order should still be adapted per customer.' },
  { id: 'ea-108', category: 'Energy Planning', question: 'A customer already has excellent insulation and a new roof, and is deciding between starting with a heat pump or solar first. What should guide this decision?', choices: ['The roadmap must always start with weatherization regardless of existing conditions', 'The order does not matter since insulation and roof concerns dictate everything', 'Their specific cost/comfort priorities and current heating fuel efficiency, since neither envelope nor roof is a blocking factor here', 'Solar must always come before HVAC in every roadmap'], answerIndex: 2, explain: 'With envelope and roof already addressed, the sequencing decision shifts to the customer\'s specific priorities rather than a fixed universal order.' },
  { id: 'ea-109', category: 'Energy Planning', question: 'Why is an "Energy Assessment" often placed as Phase 1 in a roadmap rather than jumping directly to equipment phases?', choices: ['Energy assessments are only a formality with no bearing on later phases', 'It establishes the diagnostic baseline (usage, envelope, electrical capacity) that later phases should be built on', 'Assessments are required only for financing approval', 'Skipping the assessment produces the same quality of recommendation'], answerIndex: 1, explain: 'An initial assessment provides the diagnostic foundation — usage, envelope, and electrical data — that later phased recommendations depend on for accuracy.' },
  { id: 'ea-110', category: 'Energy Planning', question: 'A customer wants to know if they must complete all four roadmap phases with Nexis. What is the appropriate advisor response?', choices: ['Tell them all four phases are contractually required together', 'Refuse to provide any single phase without commitment to all four', 'Tell them the roadmap concept does not allow for partial completion', 'Explain the roadmap is a flexible guide tailored to their goals, not a mandatory package they must complete in full'], answerIndex: 3, explain: 'The roadmap is a flexible, consultative planning tool — customers are not obligated to commit to every phase, and each step is presented as a recommended next step, not a mandate.' },
  { id: 'ea-111', category: 'Energy Planning', question: 'A customer\'s roadmap places "electrical panel review" ahead of both the heat pump and solar phases. Why might this be appropriate?', choices: ['Electrical panel review should never precede HVAC or solar work', 'Panel review is irrelevant to either HVAC or solar planning', 'If electrical capacity is uncertain, confirming it early avoids designing HVAC or solar plans around an assumption that may not hold', 'This sequencing is only relevant for commercial properties'], answerIndex: 2, explain: 'When electrical capacity is uncertain, resolving that question early prevents later phases from being designed around unverified assumptions.' },
  { id: 'ea-112', category: 'Energy Planning', question: 'How should an advisor handle a roadmap when the customer\'s situation changes significantly (e.g., they decide against buying an EV)?', choices: ['Revisit and adjust the roadmap to reflect the updated plans rather than keeping outdated assumptions', 'Keep the original roadmap exactly as-is regardless of changes', 'Refuse to update the roadmap once it has been presented', 'Cancel all future phases entirely due to any single changed plan'], answerIndex: 0, explain: 'A roadmap should be a living plan that gets updated when a customer\'s circumstances or plans meaningfully change.' },
  { id: 'ea-113', category: 'Energy Planning', question: 'What is the risk of presenting a customer with only a single "all-in-one" package instead of a phased roadmap when their budget and priorities are unclear?', choices: ['There is no risk; single packages are always preferable', 'Phased roadmaps are illegal to offer under company policy', 'It may not match their actual financial readiness or priorities, undermining the consultative, non-pressure approach', 'Single packages always cost less than phased approaches'], answerIndex: 2, explain: 'When budget and priorities are unclear, a rigid all-in-one package risks mismatching the customer\'s actual readiness, whereas a phased roadmap adapts to their situation.' },
  { id: 'ea-114', category: 'Energy Planning', question: 'A customer with a 2,200 sq ft Massachusetts home, an 18-year-old roof, poor attic insulation, oil heat, and EV plans asks for a roadmap. Which sequencing best reflects sound energy planning principles?', choices: ['Assess insulation and roof condition first (specialist referrals as needed), then evaluate heat pump and electrical capacity, then sequence solar and EV charging', 'Install solar immediately since the customer expressed interest in lower long-term costs', 'Install an EV charger first since that was mentioned last in the conversation', 'Skip all assessments and finalize every phase in a single meeting'], answerIndex: 0, explain: 'Given the aging roof, poor insulation, and planned electrification, the roadmap should start with specialist assessments (roof, insulation) and electrical capacity review before finalizing heat pump, solar, and EV charging phases.' },
];
window.EA_EXAM_BANK = EA_EXAM_BANK;
